## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a deterministic, branchless 128-bit bit-permutation using fixed masks, a common pattern in high-performance bit-twiddling tasks relevant to databases and graphics.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit word with two 64-bit halves
- Nibble-wise bit permutation via fixed masks
- Branchless, mask-and-shift composition

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 galacticShuffle128(uint128 x) {
  const uint64_t M0 = 0x1111111111111111ULL;
  const uint64_t M1 = 0x2222222222222222ULL;
  const uint64_t M2 = 0x4444444444444444ULL;
  const uint64_t M3 = 0x8888888888888888ULL;

  uint64_t lo0 = x.lo & M0;
  uint64_t lo1 = x.lo & M1;
  uint64_t lo2 = x.lo & M2;
  uint64_t lo3 = x.lo & M3;

  uint64_t hi0 = x.hi & M0;
  uint64_t hi1 = x.hi & M1;
  uint64_t hi2 = x.hi & M2;
  uint64_t hi3 = x.hi & M3;

  uint128 r;
  r.lo = lo0 | (lo2 >> 1) | (lo1 << 1) | lo3;
  r.hi = hi0 | (hi2 >> 1) | (hi1 << 1) | hi3;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to a 256-bit type with the same nibble-permutation rule?
- What are the performance implications on different architectures (SIMD lanes, interleaving with memory)?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to implement a branchless, constant-time parity on 128-bit data, a common requirement in cryptography and error-detection code. It tests knowledge of SWAR reductions and handling of multi-word integers.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR parity reduction
- 128-bit representation via two 64-bit halves
- Constant-time bit-twiddling without branches
- Correctness over both halves

## Code Example
```javascript
unsigned parity128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t hi = x.hi, lo = x.lo;
  hi ^= hi >> 32; hi ^= hi >> 16; hi ^= hi >> 8; hi ^= hi >> 4; hi ^= hi >> 2; hi ^= hi >> 1;
  lo ^= lo >> 32; lo ^= lo >> 16; lo ^= lo >> 8; lo ^= lo >> 4; lo ^= lo >> 2; lo ^= lo >> 1;
  return (unsigned)((hi ^ lo) & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt parity128 to count parity modulo 4?
- What are performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit rotation across word boundary without per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; cross-word mixing; edge k<64 vs k>=64

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi, lo; } uint128_t;
static inline uint128_t rotl128(uint128_t x, unsigned k){
  k %= 128; uint128_t r;
  if (k < 64){
    r.hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    r.lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
  } else { unsigned kk = k - 64;
    r.hi = (x.lo << kk) | (x.hi >> (64 - kk));
    r.lo = (x.hi << kk) | (x.lo >> (64 - kk));
  }
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test boundary cases k=0, 63, 64, 127?
- Could you adapt to 256-bit using 4x uint64_t without loops?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement bit-twiddling without loops, using SWAR patterns for constant-time population count.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (Small/Word-level Parallelism)
- 64-bit masks and carry-free sums
- No intrinsics or branches; deterministic performance

## Code Example
```javascript
uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to count bits in 128-bit values? 
- What are the trade-offs vs. hardware popcnt instructions in modern CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests precise bit permutation across 128-bit boundary without loops. Requires careful cross-half carry handling when shifting hi/lo.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value as {hi, lo}
- Masks for even/odd bit positions across both halves
- Cross-half carry during shifts between lo and hi

## Code Example

```javascript
// Outline (not full implementation)
uint128 swapAdjacentBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 evenMask = {0x5555555555555555ULL, 0x5555555555555555ULL};
  uint128 oddMask  = {0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL, 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL};
  uint64_t lo_e = x.lo & evenMask.lo; uint64_t hi_e = x.hi & evenMask.hi;
  uint64_t lo_o = x.lo & oddMask.lo;  uint64_t hi_o = x.hi & oddMask.hi;
  uint64_t lo_left  = (lo_e << 1);
  uint64_t hi_left  = (hi_e << 1) | (lo_e >> 63);
  uint64_t lo_right = (lo_o >> 1);
  uint64_t hi_right = (hi_o >> 1);
  uint128 res;
  res.lo = lo_left | lo_right;
  res.hi = hi_left | hi_right;
  // handle cross-half carry from lo_o/hi_o if needed
  return res;
}
```

